<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Antonio Maceo</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1845">1845</a>–<a href="/years/1896">1896</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Antonio Maceo Grajales</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Antonio Maceo was a Cuban general of the wars of independence, nicknamed the '<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze">Bronze</a> Titan'. An Afro-Cuban from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago">Santiago</a>, he rose through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ten-years-war">Ten Years' War</a> and rejected the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pact-of-zanjon">Pact of Zanjón</a> in the Protest of Baraguá in 1878. Returning in 1895, he led the invasion of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maximo-gomez">Máximo Gómez</a> and was killed in battle at San Pedro in December 1896.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/antonio-maceo.jpg" alt="Antonio Maceo" width="320" height="425" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Antonio_Maceo.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>José Antonio de la Caridad Maceo y Grajales was born on 14 June 1845 on a farm called Jobabo near San Luis, in Oriente Province outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago">Santiago</a> de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a>. His father, Marcos Maceo, was a Venezuelan farmer and trader who had fought for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simon-bolivar">Simón Bolívar</a> and moved to Santiago de Cuba in 1823; his mother, Mariana Grajales y Cuello, was a Cuban woman of Dominican descent. At sixteen he began delivering goods by mule for his father, became a farmer and trader himself, and was initiated into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freemasonry">Freemasonry</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>About two weeks after the Cry of Yara of 10 October 1868, Maceo joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ten-years-war">Ten Years' War</a> with his father and brothers, enlisting as a private. He rose quickly to lieutenant colonel and later brigadier general, fighting in more than 500 battles and recovering from more than 25 wounds, but his humble origin and the colour of his skin delayed his promotion to major general. He learned from the Dominican Máximo Gómez and took up the machete as a weapon. After the Protest of Baraguá he went abroad to raise men and arms, and from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> in 1879 he planned with Calixto García the short-lived Little War.</p><p>He settled in Guanacaste in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/costa-rica">Costa Rica</a>, where he survived an assassination attempt by Spanish agents. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-marti">José Martí</a> persuaded him to join the war of 1895, which Maceo accepted on condition that Gómez hold the highest command. Landing near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baracoa">Baracoa</a> in 1895, he became Lieutenant General, second in command, and with Gómez led two columns more than 1000 miles in 96 days, reaching Mantua at the western end of the island in October 1896. Valeriano Weyler answered the rebels' rural support with the reconcentration, in whose camps almost a third of the rural population died.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>On 7 December 1896, near Punta Brava, Maceo was cutting a fence at the farm of San Pedro with a small escort when a Spanish column opened fire. He was hit in the chest and in the head. Francisco Gómez Toro, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maximo-gomez">Máximo Gómez</a>, stayed to guard his body and was killed. The two were buried in secret, and their remains now lie at the Monumento El Cacahual south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/havana">Havana</a>, a place of pilgrimage. Monuments to Maceo stand in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago">Santiago</a> de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> and Havana.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Antonio Maceo” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Generals Máximo Gómez and <b>Antonio Maceo</b> led the fighting.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-cuba-in-arms">Republic of Cuba in Arms</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in San Luis, in what is now <a href="/countries/cuba">Cuba</a>. Died in Punta Brava, in what is now <a href="/countries/cuba">Cuba</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=20.19,-75.85&amp;year=1878" aria-label="San Luis on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2505 1416 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Antonio Maceo"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2955 1686h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2955 1686h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=20.19,-75.85&amp;year=1878">What was here in 1878? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
